        },
        "narrative": "<p>Duncan Lake is a classic Lake Trout lake (Ecological Class 1) located approximately 23 miles north of the City of Grand Marais. The lake is entirely within the BWCAW, and is most commonly accessed via a 0.25-mi portage trail from West Bearskin Lake (entry point #60). Duncan Lake is considered a native Lake Trout lake, although it was stocked on numerous occasions up until 1988. The 2018 survey of Duncan Lake was the first of two scheduled in the 2015 lake management plan to monitor the status of the Lake Trout population. The current long-range goal for the lake is to maintain a Lake Trout population with a minimum gill net catch of 1.0 fish/set (deep and shallow sets combined), with a minimum mean weight of 3.0 lb/fish. <br> \n <br> \nPast survey results for Duncan Lake have generally indicated moderate Lake Trout abundance, compared to similar lakes in the Grand Marais area. Results from the 2018 survey confirmed a healthy, self-sustained Lake Trout population remained present in Duncan Lake. The combined gill net catch (3.44 fish/set) was the highest observed to date for the lake, and was slightly above the median catch rate for this class of lake. The mean weight of Lake Trout sampled in 2018 was also slightly above average compared to other lakes; it was similar to past results from the lake, but fell short of the long-term size goal. Lake Trout natural reproduction appeared very consistent in recent years; 11 year classes were represented in the sample (age-2 through age-20), and there were no missing year classes between age-2 and age-9. The 2012 year class (age-6) appeared strongest, accounting for approximately 32 percent of the total Lake Trout catch. Growth rates of those fish appeared somewhat slow; mean length at age-6 was 14.2 inches, compared to a mean of 16.1 inches at that age for populations in similar lakes. Slower growth of younger Lake Trout was likely due to competition from an abundant Cisco population. Although Cisco may be a source of competition for Lake Trout at younger ages, the species provides a high quality forage base for larger Lake Trout, and as observed in 2018, that can result in the occasional trophy sized fish. <br> \n <br> \nPersistence of a healthy Lake Trout population within a lake requires pristine water quality, and abundant cold, well-oxygenated habitat. A temperature-dissolved oxygen profile completed in early September 2018 in Duncan Lake confirmed such conditions; optimal Lake Trout habitat (temperature <55 F, dissolved oxygen >5 ppm) was present from 28 to 90 ft deep. <br> \n <br> \nDuncan Lake continued to support an abundant Cisco population in 2018. Similar to most past surveys, the 2018 combined gill net catch of Cisco was well above average compared to similar lakes in the area. Cisco sampled in 2018 appeared larger than in past surveys (0.84 lb/fish), and could provide some angling opportunity for interested parties. Rainbow Smelt have long been established in Duncan Lake, and likely still provided additional forage for Lake Trout despite their absence in the 2018 catch. In most lakes, Rainbow Smelt are generally too small to be sampled effectively in standard gill nets. <br> \n <br> \nAdditional species sampled in Duncan Lake in 2018 included Smallmouth Bass, Green Sunfish, and White Sucker. Smallmouth Bass and Green Sunfish may be more prevalent within the lake than these results indicate; gill nets are not considered an effective gear for determining abundance of either species. White Sucker abundance appeared low compared to similar lakes in the area, but was considered normal based on past results from Duncan Lake. <br> \n</p>",
