        "narrative": "<p>It appears, on the basis of this assessment, that Duncan Lake will be able to maintain a satisfactory lake trout population  without any further stocking.  The combined catch in deep and shallow gill net sets in 2001 was near the median for the lake class, and the average size of fish collected was excellent.  Based on fin clips observed, and on aging results, all but two of the lake trout collected in 2001 had been produced naturally, since the lake was last stocked in 1988.  Growth rate for lake trout had been about average for the area.  On average fish attained a length of 14 inches at five years of age.The catch of smallmouth bass in shallow gill net sets was higher than catches observed in this gear in the past, in this lake.  The difference; however, may not have been significant, given the low number of sets, and the usual variability in smallmouth bass catches.  Several year classes were included in the catch, but no fish less than five years old were taken.  Smallmouth bass growth had been slow; six-year-old fish averaged 11.4 inches in length when collected in early August.Northern cisco were abundant, and provided an excellent forage base for lake trout in the lake.  No other game fish, and no panfish, were collected in 2001, as has usually been the case in this lake.</p>",
